            {
              "id": "de8a1b40-c281-32ad-a61b-31e5fa4aedc3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. The `getenv` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. This can lead to potential issues if the environment variable is controlled by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `getenv` function directly. Instead, use secure alternatives that are provided by the language or framework. Validate all inputs and outputs. Do not trust data from any untrusted source. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `getenv` function, consider using a secure alternative. For example, you can use `std::getenv` which is a safer version of `getenv`. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nconst char* no_signal_handling = std::getenv(\"HIPBLAS_TEST_NO_SIGACTION\");\nif(no_signal_handling == nullptr) {\n    // Handle the case when the environment variable is not set\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- cstdlib\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "737b4f3a-12ec-3d22-b044-651438c57663",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities if the environment variable it retrieves is controlled by an attacker.\n\nThe `getenv` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name passed as input. If the specified environment variable is not found, the function returns a null pointer. If the returned string is modified, subsequent calls to `getenv` return the modified string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using the `getenv` function if possible. If you must use it, ensure that the returned string is not modified and that it is used in a way that does not expose it to potential attackers. Validate the input and output of the function, and consider using safer alternatives if available.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nchar* env;\nconst char* name = \"HIPBLAS_TEST_TIMEOUT\";\nenv = getenv(name);\nif(env != NULL) {\n    // Use the value of env\n} else {\n    // Handle the case where the environment variable is not set\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if the environment variable is set before using it. This can prevent potential null pointer dereferences. However, this does not fully mitigate the risk associated with using `getenv`, and safer alternatives should be considered if possible.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `getenv` function is part of the C standard library (libc), so no additional libraries are needed for this code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4d180651-2a79-37b8-9190-3d2782403809",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities if not properly handled. The `getenv` function returns a pointer to a string that is associated with the matched environment variable name. If the environment variable is controlled by an attacker, it can lead to serious security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. If it's necessary to use them, ensure that proper error handling and input validation are implemented. In the case of `getenv`, consider the following:\n\n- Check if the returned pointer is NULL before using it.\n- Do not assume that the environment variable is always present.\n- Be aware that the returned string may be modified by subsequent calls to `getenv`, `setenv`, or `unsetenv`.\n- Be aware that environment variables can be controlled by attackers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nchar* gtest_listener = getenv(\"GTEST_LISTENER\");\nif(gtest_listener == NULL) {\n    // Handle the error, e.g., by using a default value\n    gtest_listener = \"default_value\";\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\n- Standard C Library (libc)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6ce4343b-d373-3f07-90f9-d0653653935a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of weak or non-cryptographic random number generators that can lead to predictable or easily guessable values. This can be exploited by attackers to predict the output of the random number generator, leading to security vulnerabilities.\n\nIn the given code snippet, `rand()` is a weak random number generator as it uses a linear congruential generator that can be easily predicted.\n\n```cpp\nuniquestr += alphanum.at(rand() % stringlength)\n```\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a strong cryptographic random number generator. In C++, you can use functions provided by the `<random>` library, such as `std::random_device` or `std::mt19937` (Mersenne Twister 19937 generator).\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using `std::random_device`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> distr(0, stringlength - 1);\n\nuniquestr += alphanum.at(distr(gen));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe fixed code requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "6ce4343b-d373-3f07-90f9-d0653653935a"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5f662b57-315a-3ade-a240-7db193bf0848",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of weak or non-cryptographic random number generators that can lead to predictable or easily guessable values. This can be exploited by attackers to predict the output of the random number generator, leading to security vulnerabilities.\n\nIn the given code snippet, `rand()` is a weak random number generator as it uses a linear congruential generator that can be easily predicted.\n\n```cpp\nuniquestr += alphanum.at(rand() % stringlength)\n```\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a strong cryptographic random number generator. In C++, you can use functions provided by the `<random>` library, such as `std::random_device` or `std::mt19937` (Mersenne Twister 19937 generator).\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using `std::random_device`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> distr(0, stringlength - 1);\n\nuniquestr += alphanum.at(distr(gen));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe fixed code requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "5f662b57-315a-3ade-a240-7db193bf0848"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "599884ba-1a79-3a56-b39d-07a66e18a1ef",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, \"Avoid Using Weak or Non-Cryptographic Random Number Generators\" is a vulnerability that arises when a weak or non-cryptographic random number generator is used in a context requiring a strong and unpredictable random number. This can lead to predictability in generated values, which can be exploited by an attacker to guess the next value that will be generated, leading to a breach of security.\n\nIn the given code snippet, `rand()` is a weak random number generator as it uses a linear congruential generator and does not provide cryptographic security.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a strong and unpredictable random number generator. In C++, the `<random>` library provides several such generators. For cryptographic security, consider using libraries like OpenSSL or functions like `std::random_device` and `std::uniform_int_distribution`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using `std::random_device` and `std::uniform_int_distribution`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(-2, 2);\n\nhc[i] = dis(gen);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe fixed code requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "599884ba-1a79-3a56-b39d-07a66e18a1ef"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a97d8622-6263-3acb-8f83-1f7cf386df42",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, \"Avoid Using Weak or Non-Cryptographic Random Number Generators\" is a vulnerability that arises when a weak or non-cryptographic random number generator is used in a context requiring a strong and unpredictable random number. This can lead to predictability in generated values, which can be exploited by an attacker to guess the next value that will be generated, leading to a breach of security.\n\nIn the given code snippet, `rand()` is a weak random number generator as it uses a linear congruential generator and does not provide cryptographic security.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a strong and unpredictable random number generator. In C++, the `<random>` library provides several such generators. For cryptographic security, consider using libraries like OpenSSL or functions like `std::random_device` and `std::uniform_int_distribution`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using `std::random_device` and `std::uniform_int_distribution`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(-2, 2);\n\nhc[i] = dis(gen);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe fixed code requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
